AUTOMATA  vs  PROCESS ALGEBRA
----------------------------------
[Automata Foundation]
    Classical state-machine theory (1950s–1970s).
    Objects: explicit states, transitions, morphisms.
    Semantics: algebraic, geometric, structural.

[Process Algebra Foundation]
    Syntactic calculi (CSP, CCS, ACP).
    Objects: process terms, operators, rewrite laws.
    Semantics: operational rules + bisimulation.

----------------------------------
[Automata State Space]
    Concrete, explicit S.
    Geometry is visible and manipulable.

[Process Algebra State Space]
    Implicit; emerges only after LTS expansion.
    Geometry is hidden behind syntax.

----------------------------------
[Automata Transitions]
    δ : S × A → S
    Pure structural map; algebraic invariants preserved.

[Process Algebra Transitions]
    Derived by operational semantics rules.
    Often ad-hoc, calculus-specific.

----------------------------------
[Automata Composition]
    Machine product M × N.
    Canonical concurrency operator.
    Associative, commutative (up to iso), identity exists.

[Process Algebra Composition]
    Parallel operator || or |.
    Defined by syntactic rules; not algebraically canonical.

----------------------------------
[Automata Abstraction]
    Homomorphisms h : M → N.
    Structure-preserving; invariant-driven.

[Process Algebra Abstraction]
    Bisimulation equivalence.
    Behavioral but not strictly structural.

----------------------------------
[Automata Equivalence]
    Morphism-based; algebraic compression.

[Process Algebra Equivalence]
    Bisimulation; sensitive to syntactic formulation.

----------------------------------
[Automata Concurrency]
    Emerges from product geometry.
    No extra rules needed.

[Process Algebra Concurrency]
    Requires operational semantics + synchronization laws.

----------------------------------
[Automata Modularity]
    Guaranteed by product + homomorphism algebra.

[Process Algebra Modularity]
    Depends on calculus-specific rewrite laws.

----------------------------------
[Automata Semantics]
    Algebraic, invariant-preserving, geometric.

[Process Algebra Semantics]
    Operational, syntactic, rule-based.

----------------------------------
[Automata View] 
    “State geometry first.”

[Process Algebra View] 
    “Syntax first; geometry later.”

=====================================================================
UNIFIED ALGEBRA: AUTOMATA ⊗ PROCESS ALGEBRA

    A single algebra where:
        - Automata give explicit geometry (states, transitions, products)
        - Process algebra gives syntactic operators (choice, parallel, hiding)
    The unification is achieved by mapping all process operators into
    machine-level constructions that preserve automata invariants.

=====================================================================
OBJECTS
Automata:
    M = (S, A, δ)

Process Terms:
    P ::= a.P | P + Q | P || Q | P \ L | ...

Unified Object:
    U(P) = automaton extracted from P
    U(M) = automaton itself

Thus:
    Obj(UNIFY) = { automata }  ∪  { automata extracted from process terms }

=====================================================================
MORPHISMS
Automata morphisms:
    h : M → N
    h(δ_M(s,a)) = δ_N(h(s),a)

Process algebra equivalences:
    P ~ Q  (bisimulation)

Unified morphism:
    H : U(P) → U(Q)
    defined as the *unique homomorphism* consistent with bisimulation.

Key law:
    bisimulation  ⇒  existence of a homomorphism
    homomorphism  ⇒  canonical abstraction

=====================================================================
COMPOSITION
Automata product:
    M × N

Process parallel:
    P || Q

Unified operator:
    U(P || Q)  =  U(P) × U(Q)

Thus:
    parallel composition = machine product

This is the core unification:
    process algebra’s concurrency operator is reinterpreted as
    automata’s canonical product.

=====================================================================
SYNTAX → GEOMETRY MAP
Choice:
    U(P + Q) = disjoint union automaton with initial nondeterministic branch

Prefix:
    U(a.P) = automaton with a single a-transition into U(P)

Hiding:
    U(P \ L) = automaton where transitions labeled in L become τ

Recursion:
    U(X = P) = least fixed point of U(P)

All process operators become automata constructors.

=====================================================================
SEMANTICS
Automata semantics:
    algebraic, geometric, invariant-preserving

Process algebra semantics:
    operational, rule-based

Unified semantics:
    Every process term is interpreted as an automaton.
    Every automaton is already an object of the unified algebra.

Thus:
    semantics = automata semantics
    syntax = process algebra syntax
    glue = U(·) mapping

=====================================================================
EQUIVALENCE
Automata:
    homomorphism-based abstraction

Process algebra:
    bisimulation

Unified equivalence:
    P ~ Q  ⇔  U(P) and U(Q) are homomorphically equivalent

This restores structural invariants to process algebra.

=====================================================================
CONCURRENCY
Automata:
    concurrency = product geometry

Process algebra:
    concurrency = || operator + rules

Unified:
    P || Q  interpreted as  U(P) × U(Q)

Thus:
    concurrency is geometric, not syntactic.

=====================================================================
FULL UNIFIED ALGEBRA
UNIFY = ( Obj, Morph, ⊗, +, hide, prefix, fix )

Where:

    Obj   = automata + extracted automata
    Morph = homomorphisms consistent with bisimulation
    ⊗     = machine product (parallel)
    +     = disjoint union (choice)
    hide  = label abstraction
    prefix= transition constructor
    fix   = recursion operator

This is a complete algebraic system.
